# Rank 1030 / CVE-2026-46695: BoxLite runs untrusted code in a container but
# handed that container the whole Linux capability set through
# all_capabilities(), CAP_SYS_ADMIN included. With mount powers the code inside
# can remount a read-only directory read-write and write wherever it likes
# (CWE-284). The fixed revision replaces the full set with default_capabilities(),
# an allow-list that leaves out CAP_SYS_ADMIN, CAP_NET_ADMIN, CAP_SYS_MODULE and
# CAP_BPF, and enforces the read-only mount at the hypervisor level as well.
#
# ATTENTION. The concept exists but does not reach this code, and the obvious
# binding cannot tell the two revisions apart.
#
# code.PrivilegedContainer (VYQL-CFG-010, CWE-250) is the right idea, but it is
# bound only for configuration files and JavaScript. Here the grant is Rust
# source assembling an OCI spec, which no binding covers.
#
# Matching the capability name does not work. Both revisions of
# capabilities.rs contain CAP_SYS_ADMIN exactly once: in the vulnerable file as
# an entry in the granted list, and in the patched file inside the doc comment
# that says it is excluded. A presence binding reports the patched code just as
# loudly, the same trap ranks 1013 and 1017 hit from the other direction.
# Separating them means knowing the list is used as the container's granted set
# rather than being an enumeration or prose, which needs dataflow from the
# capability set into the spec and an awareness of comments that the flat
# feature bag does not provide. A runtime library also has legitimate reason to
# name every capability, which is exactly what the vulnerable function is for.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-cost

test "rank1030: container spec is granted the full capability set"
  lang rust
  file src/guest/src/container/capabilities.rs
  reject VYQL-CFG-010
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashSet;

  pub fn all_capabilities() -> HashSet<Capability> {
      let names = vec![
          "CAP_CHOWN".to_string(),
          "CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE".to_string(),
          "CAP_FOWNER".to_string(),
          "CAP_SETGID".to_string(),
          "CAP_SETUID".to_string(),
          "CAP_NET_ADMIN".to_string(),
          "CAP_NET_RAW".to_string(),
          "CAP_SYS_MODULE".to_string(),
          "CAP_SYS_RAWIO".to_string(),
          "CAP_SYS_ADMIN".to_string(),
      ];

      names.into_iter().filter_map(|n| Capability::from_name(&n)).collect()
  }
  ```

test "rank1030: fix grants an allow-list that leaves out the mount capability"
  lang rust
  file src/guest/src/container/capabilities.rs
  reject VYQL-CFG-010
  code
  ```
  use std::collections::HashSet;

  /// Capabilities granted to container processes by default.
  ///
  /// while excluding dangerous capabilities like CAP_SYS_ADMIN (mount/remount,
  /// namespace manipulation), CAP_NET_ADMIN (network reconfiguration),
  /// CAP_SYS_MODULE (kernel module loading), and CAP_BPF.
  pub fn default_capabilities() -> HashSet<Capability> {
      let names = vec![
          "CAP_CHOWN".to_string(),
          "CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE".to_string(),
          "CAP_FOWNER".to_string(),
          "CAP_SETGID".to_string(),
          "CAP_SETUID".to_string(),
          "CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE".to_string(),
      ];

      names.into_iter().filter_map(|n| Capability::from_name(&n)).collect()
  }
  ```
